Title: How to idiomatically implement a C++26 reflection-based enum-to-string serializer using std::meta?
Tags: c++, reflection, enums

Question:
We are modernising code with macro-based enum-to-string serializers. The goal is to use C++ 26 compile-time reflection (meta::info) to automatically generate string representations of an enum class without manually maintaining duplicate string arrays or relying on the preprocessor.

What we have tried:

Extract metadata: used the new reflection operator ^^ to get meta::info of enum

Query members: used std::meta::members_of to retrieve a collection of reflection info objects for all enumerators within enum.

Filter and extract names: looped over members at compile time, filtering out non-enumerators if necessary and using std::meta::identifier_of to extract their strings.

Compile-time range evaluation: used a template for loop or string literal array initialization to build a zero-overhead look up utility.

Since C++26 expansion statements(template for ) and Reflection are separate proposals, many early compilers support reflection but haven't implemented template for loop syntax yet. What is the workaround to iterate over meta::enumerators_of?

How can we optimize this code for runtime optimization?

Current code:

#include <experimental/meta>
#include <string_view>
#include <array>
#include <optional>

// Example user enum with non-sequential values
enum class LogLevel {
    Info = 10,
    Warning = 20,
    Error = 30
};

// Generic Reflection-based serializer
template <typename T>
    requires std::is_enum_v<T>
constexpr std::string_view enum_to_string(T value) {
    constexpr auto enum_reflect = ^^T;
    constexpr auto members = std::meta::enumerators_of(enum_reflect);

    std::string_view result = "Unknown";

    // template for unrolls this loop completely at compile time
    template for (constexpr auto member : members) {
        // Ensure we are looking at an actual enumerator value
        if constexpr (std::meta::is_enumerator(member)) {
            if (value == std::meta::value_of<member>()) {
                result = std::meta::identifier_of(member);
            }
        }
    }

    return result;
}

int main() {
    constexpr LogLevel level = LogLevel::Warning;

    // Resolves perfectly to "Warning" at compile/runtime without macros
    std::string_view str = enum_to_string(level);
}

