Title: SVD of a Normal Matrix
Tags: linear-algebra, eigenvalues-eigenvectors, svd

Question:
Not a difficult question. I just want to improve my conceptual understanding.

I have the following normal matrix.

\begin{equation*}
A =
\begin{pmatrix}
-1 & 3 \\
3 & -1
\end{pmatrix}
\end{equation*}

I calculated the singular value decomposition of this harmless 2-by-2 by hand and now I have a question about the alternative methods to do this. I understand that in the SVD $A = U \Sigma V^*$, $\Sigma$ is a diagonal matrix whose diagonal entries are populated by the positive square roots of the eigenvalues of $A^*A$ and $AA^*$ in descending order, and $U$ and $V$ are unitary matrices populated by the corresponding left- and right-singular vectors of $A$, resp.

Here, $A$ is normal so the right- and left-singular vectors of $A$ would be equal. So I (incorrectly) concluded $U=V$ in the SVD of $A$. However, checking the work shows the matrix product $U \Sigma U^*$ does not generate $A$.

Calculating the singular vectors from $AA^*$, I got $\hat{u}_1 = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} (-1,1)$ associated with the largest singular value $\sigma_1 = 4$ and $\hat{u}_2 = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} (1,1)$ associated with the smaller singular value $\sigma_2 = 2$. Constructing the unitary matrix $U$ from these two unit vectors gave me

\begin{equation*}
U = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
\begin{pmatrix}
-1 & 1 \\
1 & 1
\end{pmatrix}
\end{equation*}

and

\begin{equation*}
\Sigma =
\begin{pmatrix}
4 & 0 \\
0 & 2
\end{pmatrix}
\end{equation*}

But, like I said, $U \Sigma U^*$ does not equal $A$. Calculating the columns of $V$ using the formula $\hat{v}_i = \frac{1}{\sigma_i}A^*\hat{u}_i$ on the other hand does give the correct answer.

\begin{equation*}
U = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
\begin{pmatrix}
-1 & 1 \\
1 & 1
\end{pmatrix}
\ \ \  \text{and} \ \ \
V = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
\begin{pmatrix}
1 & 1 \\
-1 & 1
\end{pmatrix}
\end{equation*}

What was my misstep here the first time I tried solving it? Is it just the case that the formula $\hat{v}_i = \frac{1}{\sigma_i}A^*\hat{u}_i$ needs to be used? Why didn't $U=V$ work? Thank you.

