Now this makes me totally baffled:

If I made a mistake, how come Claude independently made exactly the same mistake?

If I didn't make a mistake, why it is commonly claimed antenna spacing of 1/2 wavelength prevents sidelobes?

Pictures show radiation patterns with simple 2x2 antenna matrix, with antenna spacing 1/2 and 1/4 of wavelength.
In both the desired beam points directly upwards (zero angle by my definition). We can see, 1/2 wavelength gives narrower beam, but it creates equal "mirror" beam to opposite direction.

Below are my functions, or actually these are the ones made by Claude, I just removed some extra stuff and added helps and plotting.
I really appreciate some experienced to either confirm the code and my observation is true, or to point out the mistake in the code.

However, please do not even bother to present a mathematical proof for that 1/2 wavelength should not give sidelobes, unless you can show what mistake I have done in the code!

