Title: Create HTML file via rmd with custom CSS
Tags: html, css, r

Question:
I have a rmd-file to create an HTML output.

output:
  html_document:
    toc: false
    df_print: paged
    number_sections: true
    css: "own.css"

It works well.

The HTML code of the output creates a view with a max width of 940px. This seems to be based on the assigned standard CSS.

<div class="container-fluid main-container">

where the related CSS is:

.main-container {
max-width: 940px;
margin-left: auto;
margin-right: auto;
}

Now, when I try to set different values into my own.css for the class ".main-container" it has no effect.

.main-container {
max-width: 80%;
}

What I can see in the browser dev view, the standard CSS has precedence over the definitions of own.css.

How can I make sure my definitions "win"?

Answer A:
The core issue here is CSS specificity and load order. R Markdown loads its default stylesheet after your custom CSS, so when both rules use the same selector .main-container, the later default rule overwrites yours, even if you put your CSS file in the YAML css: field.

Use a more specific CSS selector

The HTML element is <div class="container-fluid main-container">.

Write your selector to target that exact combination in your own.css:

css

div.container-fluid.main-container {
  max-width: 80%;
}

This selector has higher specificity than the plain .main-container from R Markdown’s built-in styles, so your rule will take priority. Your YAML header stays exactly the same, just re-render the Rmd file.

Answer B:
Use a more specific selector in own.css:

div.main-container {
  max-width: 80%;
}

Both your original rule and the default rule use .main-container, so they have equal specificity and the one loaded last wins. Adding div makes your selector more specific, so it overrides the default regardless of their order.

Your YAML can stay unchanged. Render the document again after editing the CSS.

Alternatively, you can force the override:

.main-container {
  max-width: 80% !important;
}

The more specific selector is sufficient for the default rule you showed. R Markdown supports loading your stylesheet through the css option.

